  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1040 PM MST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nAn approaching upper level trough has brought moisture, rain and\nlots of clouds to the area this evening. The thunderstorms will\ncontinue to move off to the northeast and should clear the area a\nlittle after midnight. On Saturday a second short wave will rotate\nacross New Mexico and bring some rain showers mainly to area \nmountains Saturday afternoon and evening. I wouldn't be surprised \nif there was some rain mixed with snow above 8K, but no snow \naccumulations are expected. In addition to the mountain \nprecipitation, we will see breezy west winds across the region \nSaturday afternoon. For Sunday the upper level trough will be to \nour east while an upper level ridge will be building into the west\ncoast. This pattern will keep us dry, but cool. High temperatures\nboth Saturday and Sunday will run 3 to 5 degrees below average.\n\nOn Monday an upper level short wave ridge will dash across New\nMexico and that will keep us dry with less winds. Behind the ridge\nanother upper level trough will begin to get organized just off\nthe coast of California. As the trough approaches the area on \nTuesday we will again see a chance for rain, mainly in area \nmountains. High temperatures both Monday and Tuesday, will run \njust a degree or two below average. For the end of next week, we \nwill see yet another upper level trough begin to approach the \nregion. Right now it looks like most of the precipitation will \nstay to north of our area so there are not a lot of precipitation\nchances in the forecast. High temperatures for the end of the \nweek will be near seasonal averages.",
